# an operand that starts with - is taken as the format there; printf has no options, so here it is a usage error unless -- comes first
'-%d\n' 5
-x
-d
# length modifiers and %q are not in the conversion specifications of the standard
'%ld\n' 5
'%lld\n' 5
'%hd\n' 5
'%q\n'
# a % that is no conversion specification: the behaviour is unspecified (item 10); it writes something, we write what came before and stop with a diagnostic
'a%zb\n' 1
'%5%\n'
'%n\n'
'%p\n'
'%C\n' a
'%S\n' a
'%B\n' a
'x%dy%zz\n' 1 2
# an argument that is an empty string cannot be converted to a number: a diagnostic is required; it writes 0 without one
'%d\n' ''
# a quote and a character: the value is that of the character (the EXAMPLES of the standard: 51, 43, 45); it reports an error for what comes after the character
'%d\n' "'3" '"+3' "'-3"
'%d\n' "'ab"
'%d\n' 3 +3 -3 "'3" '"+3' "'-3"
# overflow of a floating-point argument is an error (APPLICATION USAGE: "overflow ... shall be reported as errors"); it writes inf without a diagnostic
'%f\n' 1e999
'%f\n' -1e999
# the rounding of a hexadecimal floating-point number with no digits after the point is the C library's choice
'%.0a\n' 1.5
# length modifiers are not in the conversion specifications of the standard
'%lf\n' 1.5
'%lg\n' 1.5
'%Lf\n' 1.5
# it does not read what strtod() reads: 0x10 is a hexadecimal floating-point number ("evaluated as if by the strtod() function"); an empty argument cannot be converted and needs a diagnostic; with 0 in %010a the zeros go after 0x
'%010a\n' 1
'%a\n' 0x10
'%f\n' ''
'%f\n' 1e3 1E3 2.5e-1 .5 5. -2.5 +2.5 0x10 ' 2.5' 007
